course: dictionary
lesson: 5332
title: Using a keypad safely
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A keypad is a small set of buttons with numbers, and sometimes letters. You press the buttons to type a code or a number. You will find one on a phone, a calculator, a microwave or a door lock.
explain: «Enter the code on the keypad» means press the right buttons, one at a time, to type the code in the right order. Many people use a keypad every day, at a shop, to unlock a door, or to use a lift.
explain: Look closely and you will see the numbers are not always in the same order. On a phone 1 2 3 are at the top, but on a calculator 7 8 9 are at the top. Noticing this helps you when you learn maths.
explain: Keep your PIN safe. «Cover the keypad while you type your PIN» so nobody nearby can see the numbers. Then keep the code secret and never share it, not even with friends.
fact: A keypad is a small set of buttons with numbers, used to type a code or number.
fact: «Number keypad» means a keypad that has numbers on it.
fact: Phones have 1 2 3 at the top; calculators have 7 8 9 at the top.
fact: A 4-digit PIN has 10,000 possible codes, from 0000 to 9999.
fact: Cover the keypad with your other hand so people nearby or a hidden camera cannot see your PIN.
exercise: «Cover the keypad so nobody can watch the PIN.» What is a keypad? | *A small set of buttons with numbers, used to type a code | A screen that shows how much cash you have | A card you put into a machine | A loud alarm that rings at the bank
exercise: On a phone keypad, 1 2 3 are at the top. Where is the number 7? | Bottom right | Middle of the grid | *Third row, on the left | Top left
exercise: «A 4-digit PIN has 10,000 possible codes, from 0000 to 9999.» How many possible codes are there? | 100 | *10,000 | 9,999 | 1,000
test: Why should you cover the keypad at a cash machine? | To keep the machine warm | *To keep the PIN secret from people who might watch | To make the screen brighter | To stop the card getting wet
test: Which of these uses a keypad to type a number? | A pencil case | A football | A bicycle bell | *A microwave
exam: What should you do with a keypad when you pay by card? | Show your card to the shop | Throw the card away | Shout your code out loud | *Cover it while you type your PIN
